mbk%anl433.uucp@Germany.EU.net (Matt Kennel) writes:

>There are also claims that the semantics of C are insufficient as an output
>code for some other kinds of languages, which is probably true.

>In that case, I think that one ought to attempt to identify these, and make
>extensions to C so as to make it more friendly as an intermediate language,
>and/or impose other portability requirements on 'ANDF-certified' intermediate C
>code.

>It might easier to get a few changes into C up and running rather than
>starting from scratch. 

Two things that I (and others) have missed in C as an intermediate
language for implementation of functional languages are tail-call
optimization and indirect jumps.

Tail-call optimization means that you are guarateed that if the last
thing you do in a procedure or function is to call another procedure
or function, then all the stack space that the calling function uses
is removed from the stack before the called function uses any stack
space. This ensures constant stack usage of tail-recursive (mutually)
recursive functions.

Indirect jumps are useful to implement higher order functions and the
socalled "tagless" abstract machines. Something akin to the assigned
goto of Fortran is O.K.  I don't suggest pointer arithmetic on jumps,
though I'm sure some C-hackers would love that ;-).

	Torben Mogensen (torbenm@diku.dk)


