I think you have to care about two different numbers. First one is the max_call_requests, which
you specify in one of the rpc_use_*protseq*() routines ( default is
rpc_c_protseq_max_reqs_default and this default is actually used in every case by now ). It gives
the maximum number of incoming remote procedure calls a server can handle. The handling of at
least this number of call requests by the server is guaranteed ( maybe even greater depending on
the protocol sequence ). The second important number ( the one you talk about ) is
max_calls_exec, which you specify in rpc_server_listen(). It is bordered by your hardware and
implicitly by max_call_requests. Cause the max_call_requests is always set to
rpc_c_protseq_max_reqs_default it doesn't make much sense to set max_calls_exec greater than
this, but still it is possible ( maybe more RPCs than max_call_requests are handled!!! ). The
incoming calls, which can be handled, but cannot be executed concurrently are queued. ( See OSF
DCE Application Development Reference )

Hope this helps!



