Unless it's been changed, SoftPC interprets x86 code. I've seen a remark
from someone in Insignia saying the hardest part of writing SoftPC was
emulating the hardware bugs - it really is intended to be bug for bug
compatible with the genuine hardware (PC AT, I think, but I haven't looked
at SoftPC for some years now, so they may have "upgraded the processor").
Unless my memory has been totally randomised (not impossible) this remark
was in an article where the guy was asking how some MS-DOS command worked,
and he said that he knew the PC hardware inside out, but didn't know DOS
very well. Maybe this is an improvement over knowing DOS but not understanding
the hardware :-)

The translation software is called something like Hunter XDOS. It doesn't
translate on the fly, it cross compiles (cross assembles?) x86 order codes
to native order codes once, to give you a native executable (which includes
an XDOS Int 21h emulation library), so it could act as a sort of installer
for `x86 ANDF' which was the original remark. The advertising blurb I saw
suggested that the software vendors rather than the software users would
do the translation though. The reviews I saw of it when it first came out
were complimentary about the speed of the translated executables. However,
all this was a while back as Dave Yost says, and I can't remember any more
details.

-- 
Contains no user maintainable parts.


