>rpc_c_protseq_max_reqs_default is defined as 0 in 
><dce/idlbase.h> - I would guess that 0 is interpreted as 
>infinite and this whole discussion on max_call_requests has
>been moot?  Thanks ...

The max_calls_requests parameter in reality governs the number of sockets 
allocated to handle requests for a server for a particular protocol sequence.
The default (which, as you noted above, is used regardless of the value 
specified) ends up causing only one socket (endpoint) per protocol sequence
to be allocated (NOT infinity).  Note that this is a limitation of the 
current implementation and will probably change in the future.  The number of
calls the server can keep active at one time is governed by the max_call_exec 
parameter of the rpc_server_listen call (the size of the buffer of queued
requests is derived from this parameter).  The number of incoming packets the
server can handle is the machine dependent size of the listen backlog queue on
a socket.
