A while ago I ran a program which determines the depth of instrustion
buffer, on SunPC. The result was zero, that is instructions are
executed one by one, probably by compiling each x86 instruction into
corresponding native code (1-3 native instructions for most x86 opcodes)
and doing "recompilation by request", i.e. instruction is compiled
when it is about to be executed, and compiled code is discarded when
corresponding location is overwritten. Pretty simple but space consuming.
The next speedup might be to compile the whole basic blocks if 
they were executed several times without modification.

