Mark Hickey (mhickey@ironman.qadce.osf.org) wrote:
: The number of incoming packets the server can handle is the machine
: dependent size of the listen backlog queue on a socket.

I think it's a little trickier than this.  The socket listen backlog
is (a) relevant only when the ncacn RPC protocol is being used, and
(b) matters only during transport-level connection establishment (as
opposed to all the times that multiple clients with established
connections are all trying to make calls around the same time to the
same server).

The idea of a "max calls" param to the rpc_use_protseq*() calls is
partly flawed (e.g., you don't generally really know what how many
clients are going to be using each protseq at any given moment), but
it seemed better than nothing.  It's kind of a placeholder in the
current implementation but exists against the possibility that the API
will be implemented in an environment where transport-level resources
can be explicitly allocated in a way more flexible than BSD sockets
allows them to be.  (Hint: just allocating more than one socket has
some problematical interactions with the endpoint mapper which is why
the current implementation doesn't do so.)
