Arthur Chance (arthur@Smallworld.co.uk) wrote:
: In article <CI4uwu.2KBs@austin.ibm.com>, yoder@austin.ibm.com () writes:
: > Dave Yost (Dave@Yost.COM) wrote:
: > : A while back, I thought I heard of one x86 emulation vendor
: > : who, instead of interpreting x86 code, translated it on the
: > : fly to native machine code, which they would then run instead.
: > : Yet, it seems that SoftPC is king, and they only interpret.
: > : What's the deal?
: > 
: > I'm pretty sure that SoftPC translates to native machine code
: > and then runs it (as opposed to interpreting x86 code an instruction
: > at a time).  IBM's PCSIM for AIX also follows this method.

: Unless it's been changed, SoftPC interprets x86 code. I've seen a remark
: from someone in Insignia saying the hardest part of writing SoftPC was
: emulating the hardware bugs - it really is intended to be bug for bug
: compatible with the genuine hardware (PC AT, I think, but I haven't looked
: at SoftPC for some years now, so they may have "upgraded the processor").

Whether or not SoftPC is bug for bug compatible with the genuine hardware
has nothing to do with the method of emulation-- translation vs.  
interpretation.

I think of _interpretation_ as through the cycle of fetching an
instruction, decoding it, and emulating the function on the native
machine.  This is repeated for each instruction.

I would consider _translation_ as being done in blocks-- decode an
entire block of x86 instructions and generate a corresponding block
of code on the native machine that emulates the x86 block.  Then
execute the block of native instructions.  The advantage here is that
the block can be saved and next time it can simply be executed-- no
decoding, etc. needs to done.    Of course if instruction
modification takes place the block must be 'purged' and
re-translated.

I don't know the workings of SoftPC by personal experience so I'm not
positive how they do it.

Stuart


