In article <CI528o.2prG@austin.ibm.com> larry@austin.ibm.com writes:
>
>When specifying a value using rpc_server_use_all_protseqs()
>routines, does the max_calls_requests parameter specify the 
>max *total* or the max for *each* protocol sequence? 

Each protocol sequence (if implemented to work correctly :-).

>Similarly when I call rpc_server_use_protseq() for a specific
>protocol sequence, does the max_call_requests parameter 
>effectively override the "global" number of requests allowed
>or does it govern only the number requested for that 
>particular protocol sequence?

There is no "global" number of requests allowed. It's the
combination of the number of connections accepted (tcp/ip
listen()'s backlog*number of ports, udp/ip?), the number of
requests queued (if all call executor threads are busy, default
8*number of call executor threads) and the number of call
executor threads allocated (max_calls_exec). The
max_call_requests supposed to govern the number of connections
accepted (or the number of connection requests queued) for a
particular protocol sequence if implemented.

>  I checked and
>rpc_c_protseq_max_reqs_default is defined as 0 in
><dce/idlbase.h> - I would guess that 0 is interpreted as
>infinite and this whole discussion on max_call_requests has
>been moot?

No. On most systems, it's likely SOMAXCONN.

