Ronald F. Guilmette (rfg@netcom.com) wrote:
: In article <2dl2qs$6ks@schonberg.cs.nyu.edu> dewar@cs.nyu.edu (Robert Dewar) writes:
: >
: >Even granting the need to keep distributed code proprietary, one wonders
: >if something like Gimpel's C-shroud would fill the need.

: This is *the* primary point which the ANDF proponents have consistantly
: shown a complete unwillingness and/or inability to address.

: This is also the exact point which proves that ANDF is not merely a
: commercial flop, but also a technical flop.  Adequate (and simple)
: methods for solving the exact same problem (e.g. Gimpel's C-shroud)
: already existed (or had been postulated) even before the ANDF crowd
: setup a big and expensive research effort to "study the issue".

There are also claims that the semantics of C are insufficient as an output
code for some other kinds of languages, which is probably true.

In that case, I think that one ought to attempt to identify these, and make
extensions to C so as to make it more friendly as an intermediate language,
and/or impose other portability requirements on 'ANDF-certified' intermediate C
code.

It might easier to get a few changes into C up and running rather than
starting from scratch. 

For instance, start on that damned 'noalias'.  (A key for numerical code)

E.g.

for (i=0; i<N; i++)
  {
	a[i] = /* functional depending on a[i] b[i], c[i] */
        b[i] = /* another functional on a[i],b[i],c[i] */
  }

This was recently shown as an example in the C langauge newsgroup that
the following rewrite, 

for (i=0; i<N; i++)
  {
	float ai;
        ai = /* functional depending on a[i] b[i], c[i] */
        b[i] = /* another functional on a[i],b[i],c[i] */
        a[i] = ai;
  }

speeded things up by 50%-100% on *serial* computers that the author
tested.  This is important.

Suppose we had a reasonable 'noalias'.  What should it do?  The meaning
is to guarantee that memory accesses through the following pointers are
asserted to not overlap.

So noalias is something that provides assertions about memory
accesses through sets of pointers:

for (i=0; i<N; i++)
  {
	noalias {a,b,c};   /* no overlapping between any memory
	access through these pointers */

	a[i] = /* functional depending on a[i] b[i], c[i] */
        b[i] = /* another functional on a[i],b[i],c[i] */
  }


It might be useful in regular C even.  (Wasn't there some sort of 'noalias'
proposed a while ago, but as a weird kind of attribute in a declaration,
like 'static' or 'const'.  That doesn't make any sense to me.)

Making the C garbage-collector friendly would also be a good idea.

