First:  What is ANDF?  I gather from some posts Architecture Neutral ??????

Second:  It *appears* to me to be a way of defining a "lowest common factor"
for use between various systems, to make porting applications much easier.

If this is true, has anyone heard of the PORT system?  I've only read one
article about it in either a Dr Dobbs or a Computer Language mag (I'm not at
home so can't look it up).  It appears to be something similar.  PORT runs on
top of the native environment, and provides a common backplane for programs.
You can take a PORT compiled binary, and run it on any system that supports
PORT.  Evidently everything like operating systems calls and all the C
libaries are implimented as calls to PORT to run precompile code for that
machine.  For example, the funcion cos() would not be a PORT binary, but
rather a system call to PORT, and every implementation of PORT would have a
host-specific version of cos().  

Is this something similar to ANDF?

If more interest, I'll look up the refs and post back when I get the chance.
