> 
> Mr. Patience, I hope you will forgive me for saying so, but you have just
> succeded in demonstrating your ignorance of simple "shrouding" techniques.
> 
> There is no such thing as an "unshrouder", nor is one needed.  Compilers
> do not care if they see programs containing identifiers like "Accounts_
> Payable" or identifiers like "xyzzy0037".  The code will perform the exact
> same function either way.

I doubt any ISV would consider that shrouding, certainly not adequately enough
to protect their intellectual property.

> >... and N compilers on the target machines)...
> 
> I see.  So you are suggesting that some vendor will come out with a new
> kind of machine and will produce *no* actual higher-level-language compilers
> for it, yes?  Do you really believe that this will ever happen? 

No, nor did I ever say anything of the sort. If I have an application written
in some obscure language and have one machine that has an ANDF producer for
that language then I don't need to worry if no other machine on the planet
had a compiler for that language. 

> Have you
> perhaps been residing on some other planet for the past 20 years?

This sort of comment seems to sum up the style and quality of your arguments.

> >...with one ANDF installer
> >on each target machine and N producers (1 per language) on one source machine.
> 
> I agree that if some company wanted to produce some new machine and inten-
