I have a couple of questions about ANDF.

1. Today before a vendor ships shrink-wrapped software in volume, s/he
tests extensively to ensure that customers won't wipe out profits by
swamping the tech support department with bug reports.  Vendors employ
(at best) large regression suites or (at least) large QA departments to
accomplish this. Today the (sorry) assumption that every target machine
is an IBM-compatible PC simplifies this task, but even so, the vendor
I'm familiar with uses a variety of popular clones just in case they're
different enough to reveal new bugs.

In the world envisioned by OSF for ANDF, how does a vendor ensure that
a customer won't use an ANDF back-end which the vendor never used during
testing, generating bugs which the vendor never saw? I'm
pretty sure such bugs can occur, because even if we suppose that the
back-ends are stable and bug-free, a bug in the vendor's program (such
as an uninitialized variable, an unwarranted assumption about the
packing of data, an endianness dependency, etc.) may manifest itself on
some target systems but not others.

	-- Does OSF posit only one back end for each target system?
	-- Does the vendor list on the back of the box of software the
	   particular target systems (and back-end version numbers) used
	   to test the software, with a disclaimer saying that it is not
	   warranted to work on others?
	-- Does OSF propose to publish a list of authorized back-ends,
	   with each vendor expected to test with every one, even if
	   some of them have very little market share?
	-- Does OSF expect that the vendor will test with one or more
	   "interesting" back ends and then everybody crosses their
	   fingers and hopes the number of bugs unique to untested
	   target systems is small?

2. Does OSF expect most optimization to occur before generation of ANDF,
or in the back end? If the former, then ANDF does not ensure a "level
playing field" for new languages and architectures, because there will
be more incentive to work on optimization in the ANDF-generators for
popular languages and architectures. If the latter, then the back ends
are less likely to be stable and bug-free, simply because there's
always an incentive to issue a new revision to incorporate just one
more optimization, and because (no offense to optimization technology)
revision makes bugs more likely.
