In article <PCG.93Dec8154713@frontb.aber.ac.uk>, pcg@aber.ac.uk (Piercarlo Grandi) writes:
> >>> On 7 Dec 1993 17:24:31 -0500, dewar@cs.nyu.edu (Robert Dewar) said:
> 
...stuff deleted

> Only a few vendors compete across all platforms, and they are those that
> hate the most the idea of an andf: a large part of Oracle's competitive
> advantage in the eyes of any vertical reseller that condiers it is that
> their product is available for nearly every machine; an andf would give
> the same advantage to all of Oracle's competitors for nearly free.
>

FYI, Oracle gave the ANDF team  a copy of our code to allow that team to "stress-
test" ANDF on a very large block of commercial code.  We have no particular 
interest in making basic porting as costly as possible.  Quite the contrary.

I would hope that a vertical reseller would select Oracle based on the features, 
stability and performance of our products.  We have several competitors who are 
quite capable of offering their products on the overwhelming majority of 
platforms users are interested in.

ANDF does not make software portable.  In fact, OSF has presented a seminar on 
that subject to enable the use of ANDF technology.  There are any number of non-
portable C constructs.  ANDF does not help if these constructs are used.  In addition,
every hardware vendor has done something special to make their platform more 
attractive.  Some tuning to a particular platform's features is required for a 
generic software application to compete effectively against a dedicated application.  
ANDF offers a fairly rich set of features to manage platform specific modifications 
in the context of one portable source.

By the way, the OSF-RI researchers built an ANDF version of the Oracle Server and 
several of the tools, and passed the validation tests on multiple platforms.

A related issue is "who owns the bugs?"  If ANDF offered pure portability, Oracle 
support could get a call saying "Oracle dumped core" and the dump might be for a 
processor and architecture we have never seen.  Now what?  Portability includes
a positive answer to the question: "Do you support it on this platform?"  
 
> An andf also raises the specter of lots more "piracy": as of now if you
> buy a package for architecture X and then you switch to architecture Y
> you must buy the package again or pay a rich "upgrade" fee. With andf
> you could just your rerun the installer, thus hitting the income of the
> sw vendors and making users readier to switch hw brands.
> 
This is nothing new.  Without ANDF, you buy software for some platform.  For Oracle
that may mean a price based on the capacity of the platform.  If you then upgrade 
the platform by purchasing a more capable member of the same architecture, you 
can technically copy over or reinstall the software on the new machine --possibly
violating your software license agreement.  

Alternatively, many Oracle platforms now offer user based pricing.  ANDF would 
actually enable technically what a number of our customers may be legally able to 
do already.

If the issue is enforcement of software license agreements, license management 
is a technology oriented solution with 
a lot more support of the real business  models.  There are non-
technology oriented solutions including, but not limited to, simply trusting 
most folks to do the right thing, and applying legal remedies as needed.  
(Works for MicroSoft and almost all of the rest of the PC software business.)
 
Please do not interpret this note as any kind of endorsement of ANDF by Oracle.  
This note is also not a statement of intent to support or not to support ANDF or 
any particular software licensing strategy.  
The opinions are mine, not Oracle's.

Mark H. Johnson                       
UNIX Product Line                              "Ports R Us"
