In article <rfgCHpqEE.GtD@netcom.com>,
Ronald F. Guilmette <rfg@netcom.com> wrote:
>Adequate (and simple)
>methods for solving the exact same problem (e.g. Gimpel's C-shroud)
>already existed (or had been postulated) even before the ANDF crowd
>setup a big and expensive research effort to "study the issue".

C-shroud solves a different problem.

For one thing, ANDF can be the target of multiple languages. I am
indeed aware that several languages have been implemented with C as
their compiler's output. Bleah: the result is often less than
satisfactory. I will admit that I haven't seen enough of ANDF to be
sure that it's better. But at least it's *supposed* to be. Nowhere
does C have a spec that says it should be a good notation for
programs with (say) exceptions, multithreading, and garbage
collection. And think about FORTRAN.

There are also code quality issues. In short, we'd want many
shrouders, and then we've given away some of the hoped-for benefits.





-- 
