mbk%anl433.uucp@Germany.EU.net (Matt Kennel) writes:

>Is it true that there is *one* ANDF output generator per target machine---
>independent of source language?

I believe this is the case.

>I find this hard to believe because I can't imagine any single represenation
>being good for all sorts of diverse languages---go beyond just C/C++ and
>Fortran77.  What about Ada?  Fortran/Ada 9x? What about Eiffel?  What about
>Lisp?  What about ML?  What about Maple? Matlab?  Can a single semantic model
>really do all these justice?  

This is no worse than compiling all these languages to a single
machine architecture. The difficult part of designing a system like
ANDF is that it should be sufficiently far away from a specific
machine model that the constaints of that model does not hurt
performance on dissimilar machines, but also sufficiently low level
that it doesn't rely too much on a specific source language.

As I understand it (from my limited knowledge), ANDF is fairly
low-level, but allows many annotations that e.g. tell that a
particular pointer is not aliased, or limit the range of values etc.
This way, some of the "high-level" information that is traditionally
lost when compiling to a low-level language is retained.

Ada, Fortran and C are actually very similar, so I can see no problem
in providing a common base for these. Compiling technology for LISP
and ML on stock hardware is quite good nowadays, so using the same
base as the other languages should be no problem.

I think it is intended that optimization occurs both at the high
level, before generating ANDF, in ANDF itself, and when compiling ANDF
to a specific machine. The latter is neccesary, as register allocation
and instruction scheduling can't be done before you know the specific
machine architecture.

	Torben Mogensen (torbenm@diku.dk)


