>>> On 9 Dec 93 03:57:55 GMT, mhjohnso@oracle.com (Mark H. Johnson) said:

Mark> In article <PCG.93Dec8154713@frontb.aber.ac.uk>, pcg@aber.ac.uk
Mark> (Piercarlo Grandi) writes:

pcg> Only a few vendors compete across all platforms, and they are those
pcg> that hate the most the idea of an andf: a large part of Oracle's
pcg> competitive advantage in the eyes of any vertical reseller that
pcg> condiers it is that their product is available for nearly every
pcg> machine; an andf would give the same advantage to all of Oracle's
pcg> competitors for nearly free.

Mark> FYI, Oracle gave the ANDF team a copy of our code to allow that
Mark> team to "stress- test" ANDF on a very large block of commercial
Mark> code.

Very noble of Oracle. And I must confess rather surprising.

Mark> We have no particular interest in making basic porting as costly
Mark> as possible.  Quite the contrary.

This really surprises me. Unless you mean "porting should be as easy as
possible for us, nd as hard as possible for our competitors".

Mark> I would hope that a vertical reseller would select Oracle based on
Mark> the features, stability and performance of our products.

Yes, but "availability" is perhaps the first and most important metric,
I would say, for any vertical reseller. In this Oracle has a distinct
competitive advantage (it supports more architectures than everybody
else, simply because of sheer size), while instead it is arguable that
it has an obvious one in features, stability or performance: technical
expertise is not lacking at Oracle's competitors, unlike critical mass
and hordes of porters/testers. I am really surprised the you discount
the adantaages of sprawling size:

Mark> We have several competitors who are quite capable of offering
Mark> their products on the overwhelming majority of platforms users are
Mark> interested in.

But that is not the same as _developers_. A developer tries to have as
broad a (potential) market as possible, and as profitable as possible;
that does not necessarily mean that a developer for a given vertical
application goes for the largest number of users; they may well profit
by going for niches. A developer often hates saying to a customer:
"sorry, we cannot sell our product to you because the DB engine it uses
does not run on your machine". Selling to niches is more profitable than
selling to large numbers of users, _if_ you lack capital, and that is
the normal problem with many vertical resellers.

Mark> ANDF does not make software portable.  In fact, OSF has presented
Mark> a seminar on that subject to enable the use of ANDF technology.
Mark> There are any number of non- portable C constructs.  ANDF does not
Mark> help if these constructs are used.

Yes, of course -- but if the software is _already_ portable ANDF adds an
extra dimension of portability: you need only compile it once (perhaps
adding target specific optimizations for some important targets), then
the installer takes care, and you don't need to worry about the
installer.

In theory, that is -- in practice a publisher, having produced an ANDF
product, would want to try out installing it themselves on at least some
target platforms; at this point either the ANDF is truly an andf, and it
just works, or else it is not truly an andf, and does not insulate that
much publishers and users from the target architecture, and then it is
largely irrelevant. This is why your good point:

Mark> A related issue is "who owns the bugs?"  If ANDF offered pure
Mark> portability, Oracle support could get a call saying "Oracle dumped
Mark> core" and the dump might be for a processor and architecture we
Mark> have never seen.  Now what?  Portability includes a positive
Mark> answer to the question: "Do you support it on this platform?"
 
is ultimately not very interesting; if ANDF works well, the bugs caused
by ANDF will be many less than those caused by the traditional porting
process to the target architecture, and if it does not work well, who
cares about ANDF?

Mark> By the way, the OSF-RI researchers built an ANDF version of the
Mark> Oracle Server and several of the tools, and passed the validation
Mark> tests on multiple platforms.

This is a rather interesting bit of news. Compliments to Oracle and the
OSF.

pcg> An andf also raises the specter of lots more "piracy": as of now if
pcg> you buy a package for architecture X and then you switch to
pcg> architecture Y you must buy the package again or pay a rich
pcg> "upgrade" fee. With andf you could just your rerun the installer,
pcg> thus hitting the income of the sw vendors and making users readier
pcg> to switch hw brands.

Mark> This is nothing new.  Without ANDF, you buy software for some
Mark> platform.  For Oracle that may mean a price based on the capacity
Mark> of the platform.

That is for Oracle -- other publishers have other policies. Also, a lot
of sw is published by hw manufacturers. Perhaps they don't want to
hasten their separation into sw publishing and hw manufacturing
businesses, as Sun has started to do, thanks to using the "andf" called
x86 architecture.

Mark> If you then upgrade the platform by purchasing a more capable
Mark> member of the same architecture, you can technically copy over or
Mark> reinstall the software on the new machine --possibly violating
Mark> your software license agreement.

True enough, but ANDF expands the scope into a completely new
dimension. Sw publishers already worry enough about "piracy", perhaps
they don't want to worry more. You seem to indicate, unofficially, that
Oracle is not that worried; this is an enlightened attitude, I'd wish it
were common.

Note that I write "piracy" within quotes; what I am implying is that
many sw publishers fell they would be forced by an andf to change their
licensing strategies, perhaps more towards the one adopted by Oracle,
and thus to make legal what now they would regards as "piracy".

Mark> If the issue is enforcement of software license agreements,
Mark> license management is a technology oriented solution with a lot
Mark> more support of the real business models.

I would surmise that if ANDF contained any sort of very reliable license
management or copy protection, many sw publishers would adopt it far
more enthusiastically; but then a lot of its appeal to _users_ would be
negated.

What use is an architecture neutral distribution format shrinkwrapped
application to a user if license management were to prevent it to be
freely installed on any architecture? It would be useful only to
publishers: reduce the complications of porting and have a single format
in stock good for any architecture. Well, interesting enough, but not as
wonderful to users.

All in all I see that ANDF is here and mre or less works (your news that
and ANDF version of Oracle has succeeded is rather encouraging, to say
the least), but that there is no rush by publishers to offer ANDF
"binaries" and from hw manufacturers to offer installers. They can well
argue that the reason for this is no market demand, but this goes
against all the claims that "shrinkwrapped" is very attractive to
users. Time will tell...

And Windows/NT ought to wake up anybody who is recalcitrant. Consider
this: as of now NT will be available on _all_ major workstations. Delude
yourself that it will be a roaring success :-), and that sw publishers
will offer lots of packages for it. Which binary format should they
offer those packages? I'd bet NT/86, as it would cover directly the 90%
of the NT market, and cover the smallish other segments for NT/MIPS,
NT/Alpha, ..., which are all supposed to have 86 emulators, even if
slowish.

NT sw publishers are all the same that publish for Windows 3 and so on,
and they are used to huge-colume/low-margin publishing in a large market
based on a single binary standard, and I doubt they would feel any
qualms about ANDF.

Perhaps OSF should take ANDF and offer it to Microsoft for NT: much
larger volume than Unix, perhaps, and it would neatly solve the problem
of the sluggishness of the emulators. Would that problem be worth
distributing all NT packages in ANDF format, that is also to x86
customers?  Probably not. But perhaps ANDF/NT would cut to two the
binary formats, to NT/86 and NT/ANDF. Would it be worth it?


