lindsay+@cs.cmu.edu (Donald Lindsay) writes:

>For one thing, ANDF can be the target of multiple languages. I am
>indeed aware that several languages have been implemented with C as
>their compiler's output. Bleah: the result is often less than
>satisfactory. I will admit that I haven't seen enough of ANDF to be
>sure that it's better. But at least it's *supposed* to be. Nowhere
>does C have a spec that says it should be a good notation for
>programs with (say) exceptions, multithreading, and garbage
>collection. And think about FORTRAN.

The top 3 complaints I have about using C as an intermediate language
are:

1. Compilation time.
2. Debugging problems.
3. The optimizer knows nothing about garbage collection. 
(Even the instruction scheduler should know a little about garbage
collection.)

I think this is similar to the list you would get from most Cedar, SRC
Modula-3 and Sather users.

(For numerical code, loss of aliasing information also matters.  The
current Cedar compiler generates C code that runs in a multithreaded
environment without problems, as does SRC Modula-3.  Exception handling takes a
performance hit, but that's not that major, based on profiles I've seen. I've
seen some other minor losses in code quality, but those seemed to be due
more to inattention by C compiler writers to features they thought
should be infrequent.  I would expect ANDF with a nonC front-end to
share that problem.)

It seems to me that ANDF probably makes point 2 worse.  Can anybody
verify that it helps with the other two?  (I assume it does fix the
problem with aliasing information, but I suspect that's secondary
for many applications.)

Hans-J. Boehm
(boehm@parc.xerox.com)
