In article <rick.755426518@digibd>,
Rick Richardson <rick@digibd.digibd.com> wrote:
> AT&T leveled the field when C++ first came out by generating C
> code as its intermediate language.  Once the langauge became
> popular, native compilers became available.  I don't see the
> need for ANDF to perform this function when the C language
> already can do it.

Well, sure, C can play the same kind of role. But it hasn't been
designed for it and hence poses some serious problems as soon as your
language doesn't fit the C way of living.

Ex: any language with garbage collection has to have a pretty good
control of what is where (it has to know what is a pointer and what
isn't, basically, but it generally means much more than that). This is
a BIG problem with C, because C doesn't push any information along
with spilled registers on the stack: the stack is totally typeless and
it's pretty hard to get back the type of its componenents !
And anyway, even if you could do it easily, it is compiler dependent
and access to the registers (which are part of the roots) poses the
same kind of problems. If you want your C code to be portable...good
luck.

sml2c avoids the problem by using a fixed set of variables
("registers") declared globally and pushes frames on its own
explicitely handled "stack" (it's a heap). It is highly portable, but
twice as slow as assembly (and as hard as assembly to generate)

I don't know how easy it gets with ANDF, tho. I'm afraid it would be
just as trivial !


	Stefan
