monnier@di.epfl.ch (Stefan Monnier) writes:

[Problems with using C as intermediate language for garbage collected
source language, e.g. finding pointer locations in stack(s).]

>sml2c avoids the problem by using a fixed set of variables
>("registers") declared globally and pushes frames on its own
>explicitely handled "stack" (it's a heap). It is highly portable, but
>twice as slow as assembly (and as hard as assembly to generate)

I think sml2c is in the minority here.  By doing this, it gets space
usage guarantees that are stronger than those provided by almost all
other language implementations, and it can probably afford to be
a bit sloppier about allocating short-lived objects.  If you are willing
to settle for the customary lack of guarantees (as with all C or Lisp
implementations that I know of), you can conservatively scan the stack
for bit patterns that look like pointers.  This is done by the SRC
Modula-3 compiler, the Sather compiler, AKCL, Bartlett's Scheme
compiler, etc., and even by some compilers that generate native code
(e.g. D-machine Cedar, and SRC Modula-2+, I believe.)

This avoids the factor of 2 execution time cost, but it still requires
minimal support from the optimizer (including the instruction scheduler)
to guarantee safety.  Such support happens to be accidentally provided
by some C compilers (e.g. lcc, or most compilers without optimization,
or gcc with suitable gcc-specific tweaking of the generated C code).
Based on a quick look at some of the documentation, I see no indication
that ANDF installers can be expected to support either generation of
pointer location information, or to guarantee this weak form of safety.


Hans-J. Boehm
(boehm@parc.xerox.com)
