Apologies for following my own article, but judging from the mail I got it
looks like I'd better explain it. oh well, this is the net, after all...

>There is already an ANDF of sorts in wide usage, and there already are
>or will be implementations of it for Alpha, Mips, PowerPC, sparc, etc:
>80386 assembly.

Of course SoftPC on the Mac also belongs in the list, however I was focusing
on the fact that most modern RISCs are trying to break into the PC market
by emulating DOS machines.

I don't know how anyone managed to interpret that I was suggesting
x86 be considered for the standard ANDF. That's obviously absurd.
My point is that x86, specifically DOS/win3 binaries, have already
become a de facto ANDF as far as the market is concerned.

Besides, in a sense there is NO SUCH THING AS ANDF. What everyone is calling
ANDF is itself an instruction set architecture; if someone built hardware to
execute it, then it wouldn't be architecture neutral anymore now would it?

My view on ANDF is that relative to other approaches it is pretty pointless.
ANDF tries to handle in one place something that is already being handled in
pieces from every other direction. We have easily retargetable compilers. We
have language and library standards (and people are finally using them!) and
things like POSIX. These trends basically squeeze ANDF out of any useful
place in the universe. Implementing ANDF for your chip is not going to be
much cheaper than implementing ANSI C, POSIX, and GCC for your chip. And
don't forget the extra royalties you get for supporting more hosts at a
given site!

