In article <2eakvr$is4@news.parc.xerox.com> boehm@parc.xerox.com (Hans Boehm) writes:

   I think sml2c is in the minority here.  By doing this, it gets space
   usage guarantees that are stronger than those provided by almost all
   other language implementations, and it can probably afford to be
   a bit sloppier about allocating short-lived objects.  If you are willing
   to settle for the customary lack of guarantees (as with all C or Lisp
   implementations that I know of), you can conservatively scan the stack
   for bit patterns that look like pointers.  This is done by the SRC
   Modula-3 compiler, the Sather compiler, AKCL, Bartlett's Scheme
   compiler, etc., and even by some compilers that generate native code
   (e.g. D-machine Cedar, and SRC Modula-2+, I believe.)

You seem to focus on languages that are statically typed,
implementations that are noncommercial, or implementations that
generate C code.  It is quite natural for these to scan stacks for
pointer-like bit patterns.  Indeed, they typically have no viable and
cost-effective alternative.

On the other hand, you don't mention Common Lisp implementations
specifically except for AKCL (which is noncommercial and generates C
code).  Are you certain that Harlequin, Franz, Lucid, Symbolics,
Venue, Apple, Gold Hill, CMU, and CLISP actually employ bit-pattern
scanning for their stacks?  Otherwise, your sweeping generalization
may mislead people into thinking that "Conservative GC can't be so
terrible because everybody's doing it."
--
        Lawrence G. Mayka
        AT&T Bell Laboratories
