  Todd Whitesel posts:

> Apologies for following my own article, but judging from the mail I got it
> looks like I'd better explain it. oh well, this is the net, after all...

  Indeed.  Clarity is sometimes a rare commodity.

> >There is already an ANDF of sorts in wide usage, and there already are
> >or will be implementations of it for Alpha, Mips, PowerPC, sparc, etc:
> >80386 assembly.
> 
> Of course SoftPC on the Mac also belongs in the list, however I was focusing
> on the fact that most modern RISCs are trying to break into the PC market
> by emulating DOS machines.

  Emulation is merely a technical solution for the problem that most
  applications are offered on a very limited number of platforms.  I don't
  count it a feature that I have to maintain MS-DOS in addition to my
  system's native operating system in order to be able to use such an
  application.  The reality is that this mode of operation is unacceptable
  to many corporate users, who demand SIMPLE operating environments for their
  people and are willing to settle for the "lowest common denominator"
  instead of the "best solution", whatever that might be.  Are you prepared
  to give up ALL other architectures?

> I don't know how anyone managed to interpret that I was suggesting
> x86 be considered for the standard ANDF. That's obviously absurd.
> My point is that x86, specifically DOS/win3 binaries, have already
> become a de facto ANDF as far as the market is concerned.

  Hardly "architecture-neutral", but true.  We CAN do better.

> Besides, in a sense there is NO SUCH THING AS ANDF. What everyone is calling
> ANDF is itself an instruction set architecture; if someone built hardware to
> execute it, then it wouldn't be architecture neutral anymore now would it?

  You're reaching for this one.  In a world where CPU design has put so
  much effort into optimizing for a lean instruction set, it's highly
  unlikely that anyone would be interested in defining a CPU around the
  complex abstractions embodied in ANDF.

> My view on ANDF is that relative to other approaches it is pretty pointless.
> ANDF tries to handle in one place something that is already being handled in
> pieces from every other direction. We have easily retargetable compilers. We
> have language and library standards (and people are finally using them!) and
> things like POSIX. These trends basically squeeze ANDF out of any useful
> place in the universe. Implementing ANDF for your chip is not going to be
> much cheaper than implementing ANSI C, POSIX, and GCC for your chip. And
> don't forget the extra royalties you get for supporting more hosts at a
> given site!

  I beg to differ (and not because of my work affiliation -- I work on a
  completely different project).  The simple fact is that even WITH these
  pieces, the bulk of software applications are available for only two
  platforms, neither of which claims POSIX compliance.  (I'm one of 4M
  users of the #3 personal computer and, of necessity, I have to maintain
  an MS-DOS disk partition and an x86 co-processor.)

  Software publishers would LOVE to be able to focus on ONE platform and
  ONE distribution format.  To the extent that they can ignore the rest of
  us, they can do so with impunity.  This is NOT necessarily a good thing.

  Computer users, however, choose their hardware and software systems to
  meet their specific needs and they want to have a wide variety of
  applications from which to choose.  Most of them have NO interest in
  having to compile each application to run in their own environment, as
  has long been the case in the UNIX community.

  I see ANDF as an enabling technology that will provide its greatest
  benefit to the multitude of both (numerically) "smaller" platforms and
  software publishers.  By making a modest investment in standard API
  libraries and an ANDF installer, each vendor would gain instant access to
  a body of ANDFed software, of which ORACLE and WingZ are important examples.

  In turn, software publishers gain access to a variety of smaller markets
  that might not make sense individually but, taken together, could be very
  lucrative.  It is NOT the case that most non-MS-DOS PC or workstation
  users will be willing to put up with MS-DOS emulation in order to gain
  access to such applications.  They shouldn't even be asked to.

  I use OSF/1 at work and AmigaDOS at home.  There are thousands of
  applications available for each, but few that are available for both.
  I'd like to be able to have the best of both worlds, and I believe that
  ANDF could become a significant factor in making that possible.

