Johnathan's reply is exactly right.  I only wish to point out that when 
implementing a strategy depending on cross-cell auth, you need to think out
carefully your access scheme.  Access to DCE objects; be they files in DFS,
CDS directories and objects, or whatever; is controlled by ACL's.  The 
security system is cognizant of foreign-cell access via a local principal, 
and treats such accesses differently from same-cell access via the principal.
Accesses through local principals from foreign cells are governed by the 
foreign_*, mask_obj, any_other, and unauthenticated entries in the ACL's
on objects.  These entries allow access control to be specified at a 
fine level of granularity (eg: a particular user in a particular foreign cell,
a user in a particular group in a particular foreign cell, all users in a 
particular foreign cell, etc.), but the price is the administration of the
 ACL's.  This subject is covered pretty well in the DCE User's Guide Chapter 4.

