dewar@cs.nyu.edu (Robert Dewar) writes:

>Adding to Stefan's note about limitations of C as a universal language, there
>is also the problem of lack of support for exceptions and checked
>arithmetic.

I'm not sure whether checked arithmetic is a big deal.  A high quality
implementation would probably have to do enough analysis to eliminate
most of the checks anyway.  A purely C language check requires on the
order of 3 or 4 boolean operations on the 3 sign bits, plus a test.  (E.g.,
signed addition overflow occurs iff the two arguments have the same sign bit,
and the result sign is different.)  And if you know that the C compiler is gcc,
you can generate the appropriate assembly code for the architectures you know
about.  Does anybody have any measurements as to what you really lose there?

