dave@blackjoke.bsd.uchicago.edu (Dave Griffith) writes:

>In article <2el49dINN638@network.ucsd.edu> mbk%anl433.uucp@Germany.EU.net (Matt  
>Kennel) writes:

>> There are also claims that the semantics of C are insufficient as an output
>> code for some other kinds of languages, which is probably true.

>Not "probably".  Manifestly and utterly.  There are about a half-dozen  
>important things required for non-C languages that simply cannot be done  
>efficiently when compiling by way of portable C.  My list includes GC,  
>arithmetic with traps, non-local exceptions, and (god help us) BCD arithmetic.
>More stuff can only be done if the target C compiler is quite smart indeed  
>(bit-rotates, for example).

Some of these don't seem like a big deal.
My guess is that BCD arithmetic can be moved into the run-time system
with minimal performance loss.  Arithmetic traps can be handled with
some overhead, or by assuming gcc like facilities and a tiny amount of
architecture specific code.  I have yet to see evidence that these are
addressed by ANDF (with the exception of arithmetic traps).  And I have
looked at some descriptions.

...

>> Making the C garbage-collector friendly would also be a good idea.

>A garbage-collector friendly C would require breaking _huge_ amounts of  
>existing code.

No.  Guaranteeing the safety of a conservative garbage collector is
a minimal added requirement on the code generator.  With considerably
more work, you could guarantee very precise identification of pointers,
assuming "well-behaved" source code.  In either case, I don't think
anybody would advocate requiring all C code to run with a garbage
collector.  It's sufficient to make suitably generated code safe with
a collector.  (In the case of a fully conservative collector, "suitably
generated" is basically implied by "strictly conforming ANSI C".
Empirically, the vast majority of hand-written C programs are
"suitably generated".)

The situation in ANDF seems to be principally the same, except that
it appears to be missing some of the hooks provided by gcc.  (Please
correct me if I'm wrong.)

