Of course from one point of view, ANDF technology is completely redundant,
since we already have the necessary technology: C as the distribution medium
and C compilers as the installers.

Of course this does not address the issues of keeping proprietary software
proprietary, which is really what ANDF is all about, so it is a little
ironic that "ANDF has been a technical success, but not a commercial
success".

Even granting the need to keep distributed code proprietary, one wonders
if something like Gimpel's C-shroud would fill the need.

P.S. this is awfully heavily cross posted! Since I read it on comp.lang.ada
I will ask an Ada-relevant question, namely would there be any value in
an Ada-shroud with similar intentions to the C-shroud (thoroughly mask
and disguise the intention of the source).

Note: this is not the Robert Dewar who works on GCC stuff, and therefore
would not for a moment countenance discussions of how to keep software
proprietary, but rather his evil twin brother :-)


