In article <yostCHLst5.KoA@netcom.com> Dave@Yost.COM (Dave Yost) writes:

   One nice side effect if ANDF succeeds might be that it could
   ease the stranglehold of C and C++.

Absolutely.  In fact, it makes for a "level playing field" where the
technical merits of innovative languages like Eiffel and Dylan can be
judged on their own, without worrying about availability of compilers.

   BTW, I fervently hope that the ANDF developers are giving serious
   consideration to standardizing an API for incremental use of the
   code generators and linkers so that dynamic programming
   environments and debuggers can utilize ANDF code generators in an
   incremental-compiling style.

The ANDF technology itself supports such incremental compilation and
linking today.  The problem is with the very last step in the
process-- actually loading the binaries (.o) into the running image.
ANDF itself cannot solve that problem, since ANDF depends on the
binary linkers and loaders on each machine.  I agree with you heartily
that standardizing dynamic linking and loading would be a big step
forward.

