>Of course another way of generating compilers for innovative languages is
>to make new front ends for GCC, but then of course you can't sell them
>and make lots of money! As I noted before, a lot of what ANDF is about is
>duplicating existing open technology in a manner that allows it to be kept
>proprietary. That's not in itself objectionable, unless you follow Richard
>Stallman's line of thinking to its ultimate conclusion, but it's worth
>being aware of this fact. At one point, someone enquired whether FSF would
>be doing an ANDF backend for GCC. THe answer of course is that FSF is
>certainly not interested in such a project, but that doesn't stop someone
>else doing it.

Robert, did you really mean to write such flame bait?  Is your asbestos
suit functional?

Biting my tongue, I couple of points/corrections/opinions:

  Writing an ANDF backend for GCC would allow GCC-compiled software to run
  on any vendor's machine which has the requisite "ANDF Loader".  I don't
  see ANYTHING in this capability that goes against the FSF principals.
  Maybe FSF has some other reason to not be interested in constructing such
  a backend, but it is unclear what that is.

  Robert's statement that ANDF is about "duplicating existing open technology
  in a manner that allows *it* to be kept proprietary" is ambiguous.  Of
  course ANDF enables a software vendor the ability to keep their *proprietary*
  software *proprietary*.  Nobody is trying to keep duplicated "existing open
  technology" proprietary as the "it" (my emphasis, not Robert's) in the
  sentence implied (perhaps unintentionally).
