In article <edm.170.0014990E@wrs.com> edm@wrs.com (Ed McClanahan) writes:
>  Writing an ANDF backend for GCC would allow GCC-compiled software to run
>  on any vendor's machine which has the requisite "ANDF Loader".  I don't
>  see ANYTHING in this capability that goes against the FSF principals.
>  Maybe FSF has some other reason to not be interested in constructing such
>  a backend, but it is unclear what that is.

"The FSF" is not a homogenious organization, but many, perhaps most,
people associated with the FSF believe that the presence of a scheme
like ANDF does nothing to encourage vendors to distribute their software
in source form.  To the extent that it provides an alternate technical
solution to problems that might otherwise require such distribution, it
actually discourages it.

Thus it goes against the basic goals of the FSF.

Technically, there doesn't seem to be much of a point in writing a GCC
backend that generates ANDF since GCC already has a backend for the
vast majority of machines, so this doesn't at all seem to enhance the
portability of GCC.  Instead, it would help "software hoarders" by 
letting them use GCC to compile their programs for ANDF.  That's why
many within the FSF are not comfortable helping such projects.


