>>> On 7 Dec 1993 17:24:31 -0500, dewar@cs.nyu.edu (Robert Dewar) said:

Robert> As I noted before, a lot of what ANDF is about is duplicating
Robert> existing open technology in a manner that allows it to be kept
Robert> proprietary.

Actually the main problem of ANDF is that is promises to make a lot of
sw & hw technology _less_ "proprietary". That is its main problem.

ANDF as it exists is an interesting attempt to distribute sw in some
sort of intermediate compiler oriented representation; there is nothing
technically wrong with that; I read one that some people have separated
the from and back end parts of GCC, and RTL is about as plausible an
andf as ANDF itself.

The main problem with ANDF is of course the marketing implications: the
last thing the main workstation hw & sw vendors want is any sort of
universal distribution format. As of now each machine defines a market
segment and each sw vendor for worstations have carved their own little
nice niche; some people dominate one segment and some people dominate
another. Any andf risks introducing lots more competition across the
board, and by destroying segmentation to reduce prices and margins.

Only a few vendors compete across all platforms, and they are those that
hate the most the idea of an andf: a large part of Oracle's competitive
advantage in the eyes of any vertical reseller that condiers it is that
their product is available for nearly every machine; an andf would give
the same advantage to all of Oracle's competitors for nearly free.

An andf also raises the specter of lots more "piracy": as of now if you
buy a package for architecture X and then you switch to architecture Y
you must buy the package again or pay a rich "upgrade" fee. With andf
you could just your rerun the installer, thus hitting the income of the
sw vendors and making users readier to switch hw brands.

An andf is also a bad move for the dominant hw market players: as the
DRA person that originated this thread remarked, an andf would
substantially lower the barriers to entry in the workstation market, as
it would reduce to near zero the cost of building up a vast portfolio of
portings (I seem to remember that IBM spent _billions_ in porting fees
and expenses to build up a large catalog for the RS/6000), which is
vital to gain market acceptance. No major hw player is ready to regale
such an advantage to upstarts (except perhaps for Sun, and even then
half bakedly).

In practice the only people that could benefit from an andf are the end
users; but ANDF is being developed by a sw/hw producers' association,
which is rather curious and hazard fraught situation, and to succeed it
ought to be adopted by the same people (the main hw and sw suppliers)
that currently dominate the status quo and have reason to fear the most
from any change in it.


Naturally there is one last small but vital point: even if hw and sw
vendors in the workstation market have nothing to gain from an andf, and
a lot to lose, they must contemplate introducing it to stave off losing
their customers to one market where there is one de facto andf much
beloved by users and that largely dominates hw and sw sales by volume
and in dollars: the .COM/.EXE "andf".

Workstation sw and hw vendors are between a rock and a hard spot: if
they do support ANDF they risk introducing a lot of margin depressing
competition in the workstation market, if they don't they risk seeing
the workstation market eaten away by PC sales (can you say Windows/NT?),
where an "andf" exists and is much beloved by users because it engenders
a lot of competition in both hw and sw and thus low prices and huge
volumes.

The history of companies that have carved themselves nice little
small-volume and high-margin niches is always the same: they will go
under or at least lose colossal amounts of money before they even
consider switching to a low-margin and high-volume (see DEC, IBM, Compaq
and Apple for late demonstrations of the principle) style of operation.

I think that therefore it's fair to assume that ANDF will remain on the
shelf, kept there as a last ditch defensive move against Windows/NT,
until it's way too late.


